# BENEATH THE KEYWORDS: the declaration words.
#
# Braid's declaration layer is a fixed, name-first surface — `def name =
# body` and kin — over an OPEN TABLE of DECLARATION WORDS, each carrying
# a `=Dict>` manifest.  The keyword is kept deliberately: it marks "an
# act on the dictionary" as against "a morphism", which is the phase
# distinction, visible.  What this file shows is the other side of that
# surface — the words themselves, called by a program.
#
# There are no parsing words and there never will be.  A keyword's word
# receives ALREADY-PARSED arguments — Code, Str, a TypeRep — so a file
# can be read without being run, and hygiene stays a property of the
# representation rather than a discipline.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The table, read off the words themselves
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every keyword line is parsed, whole, into a CALL of an ordinary word:
#
#     def f = body            ≡  [body] "f" defW
#     type Pair = Int Int     ≡  ⌜Int Int⌝ "Pair" typeW
#     import "util.braid"     ≡  "util.braid" importW
#
# and the ten words are ordinary words, with the manifest in the arrow.
def showsType = (w -> w ; typeOfWord ; (showType | (s -> s)) ; merge)
def arrowOf   = (w -> w ; showsType ; (t -> w " : " ; cat ; _ t ; cat))

"-- the declaration words --" ; print
"defW"   ; arrowOf ; print
"typeW"  ; arrowOf ; print
"theoryW" ; arrowOf ; print

# THE TWO THAT READ THE WORLD say so in the grade.  This is the one
# place IO happens before anything is checked — the loader resolves a
# path and reads a file — and `=Dict IO>` is the manifest of exactly
# that.  Eight of the ten are `=Dict>`.
"importW" ; arrowOf ; print
"tableW"  ; arrowOf ; print

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The same declaration, written both ways
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
def square = dup ; *

# ...and the call the line above is parsed into, written out.  `[body]`
# is a typed quotation in SOURCE and the word takes `Code`, so a program
# supplies the erasure the way it supplies any Code — `getCode`.
([dup ; *] ; getCode) "squared" ; defW

"-- one declaration, two spellings --" ; print
5 ; square  ; print                              # 25
5 ; squared ; print                              # 25, the same word

# WHERE IT LANDS.  A declaration line runs above main and BELOW the
# module's written defs, because that is the dictionary a compile-time
# word sees: the one so far.  So `squared` is a word main may call, and
# a written `def` above it may not — `Unknown primitive: squared` — for
# the same reason a `functor` must be runnable before its first use.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. PROGRAMMATIC DECLARATION: a loop that defines words
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A top-level line is a DECLARATION LINE when its GRADE says so — when
# what it does carries `Dict`.  Not when it happens to name `defW`: the
# loop below names it three levels down, inside `declare`, and the grade
# is the only thing that knows.  That is what a manifest is for.
def orNil   = ((c -> c) | drop ; nil) ; merge
def nameFor = (n -> "add" (n ; toStr) ; cat)
def bodyFor = (n -> "_ " (n ; toStr) ; cat ; _ " ; +" ; cat ; parse ; orNil)
def declare = (n -> (n ; bodyFor) (n ; nameFor) ; defW)

[(acc n -> n ; declare ; acc)] 0 (1 2 3 ; pack) ; fold ; drop

"-- three words nobody wrote --" ; print
10 ; add1 ; print                                # 11
10 ; add2 ; print                                # 12
10 ; add3 ; print                                # 13

# They are DEFS, not a trick: the checker has them, with schemes, and
# `:defs` lists them in a session.  Here the reflection words say the
# same thing — which is the read side of the dictionary, the declaration
# words being the write side.
"-- and the checker has them --" ; print
"add1" ; arrowOf ; print
"add2" ; arrowOf ; print

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. THE TABLE IS OPEN: a keyword a library adds
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `keyword <name> = <a declaration word>` binds a keyword to a word, and
# from that line on `<name> x = …` is a declaration.  It is itself a
# declaration line, so it is a row of the table like the others.
#
# `testW` is a declaration word the kernel ships with NO keyword of its
# own — which is what makes the binding worth having.  It registers a
# runnable check, and a check runs at module start, beside the laws: a
# claim a module makes about itself is worth as much as the moment it is
# decided, and that moment is before anything prints.
keyword test = testW

test squareIs25 = (5 ; square) (25) ; eq? ; verdict
test addsUp     = (2 3 ; +) (5)    ; eq? ; verdict

"-- both checks already ran --" ; print

# A check that answers `false` is a module error, named:
#
#   test wrong = (5 ; square) (26) ; eq? ; verdict
#     test 'wrong' fails: a `test` runs at module start and must answer
#     `true`
#
# ...and one that is not `• ⇒ Bool` is refused at the declaration, the
# same courtesy a law gets:
#
#   test wrong = 5
#     test 'wrong' must be a program with type `• ⇒ Bool`, but is • ⇒ Int

# AND THE WORD MAY BE THE MODULE'S OWN.  A declaration word is any word
# at `Code Str =Dict> •`; here is one written in Braid, which declares
# the word it is given AND the word composed with itself.
def twiceW = (c n -> c n ; defW ; (c c ; append) (n ; _ "Twice" ; cat) ; defW)
keyword double = twiceW

double bump = _ 1 ; +

"-- one keyword line, two words --" ; print
2 ; bump      ; print                            # 3
2 ; bumpTwice ; print                            # 4, the word composed with itself

# `keyword` refuses to redefine one of the language's own:
#
#   keyword def = testW
#     `keyword def` is refused: def is already a keyword of the
#     language, and a keyword names one declaration word.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. What a declaration line may and may not be
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A declaration line runs at CHECK TIME, above main and below the defs —
# the dictionary a compile-time word sees is the dictionary so far.  So:
#
#   * it must take nothing and leave nothing (`• =Dict> •`), because
#     lifting it out of main would otherwise change what main does:
#
#       ([dup] ; getCode) "q" ; defW ; 1
#         a DECLARATION LINE is run at check time, above main, so it
#         must take nothing and leave nothing (`• =Dict> •`)
#
#   * it may not touch the world.  Reading a file before anything is
#     checked is the LOADER's, and `import` and `table` are its words:
#
#       "hello" ; print ; ([dup] ; getCode) "q" ; defW
#         a DECLARATION LINE runs at check time, before main, so it may
#         not touch the world: this one is `=Dict IO>`
#
#   * and of the ten words, `defW` is the one a program may call.  The
#     other nine declare things the module's own defs are checked
#     AGAINST, and those are checked above the program that would
#     declare them:
#
#       "Mon" "op : a a ⇒ a" ; theoryW
#         `theoryW` is a declaration word, and a program may not call
#         this one yet … Write the keyword line.
#
# `Dict` itself is a resource the loader threads, and a program cannot
# discharge it — there is no `Dict` to seed one with and no `unDict` to
# open one with, because the name may not be declared at all.  `:doc
# Dict` says so.
